{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Provide a persistent SSH tunnel from node2 loopback TCP 9082 to the inventory endpoint on node1 loopback TCP 9081.","status":"satisfied","summary":"After sequential node restarts, node1 served the expected document on 127.0.0.1:9081 and node2 served it through an enabled, active SSH tunnel on 127.0.0.1:9082. Changed boot identities and recovered listeners directly demonstrate persistence.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a08bd35b6bf944fe","cmd-1b3487aa8b8c4800","cmd-da27dd691b224348","cmd-4067c549abb24a44","cmd-94944681c86043fa","cmd-3b3d568b680a4bf5","global:after_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_prepare:node2:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Prevent cluster hosts from directly accessing either application listener.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both listeners were observed bound exclusively to loopback. Cross-node requests to node1:9081 and node2:9082 were blocked before and after restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-573844756b184d9e","cmd-310ef0bc79884b95","cmd-94944681c86043fa","cmd-3b3d568b680a4bf5","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Recover the tunnel automatically after a brief SSH connection interruption without manual recreation.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The tunnel was healthy with PID 2371, its node1 SSH session was terminated, and it automatically recovered as PID 2421 with the restart count incremented and the expected HTTP document available again.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b5e5dd5e7be14b87","cmd-b19634f1332043a3","cmd-df52bd8376f745e9"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Preserve the supplied status document and application under /srv/legacy2/ssh-local-service-tunnel.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Final hashes on both nodes exactly match the preparation baseline, including after the restart tests, and the preserved status document was served successfully.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","seeded-state-node2","cmd-1b3487aa8b8c4800","cmd-4067c549abb24a44","cmd-94944681c86043fa","cmd-3b3d568b680a4bf5"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on RHEL 9.8 with kernel 5.14.0-687.20.1.el9_8. The implementation used installed RHEL OpenSSH, Python 3, and systemd components, and final package-health checks passed.","evidence_ids":["global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_prepare:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform","cmd-8899128c96134f92","cmd-aab43a7d9b5c48d8","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.9,"summary":"Final systems had healthy package state, no failed services, and only the required loopback listeners. Two hygiene issues remain. First, four evidence scratch files were abandoned on node2: /tmp/tunnel-status.out from cmd-bd1cbdfd4a184040/cmd-22688f91087941c8, /tmp/recovered-status.out from cmd-df52bd8376f745e9, /tmp/node1-restart-recovery.out from cmd-661a0daf520e4d53, and /tmp/post-restart-status.out from cmd-4067c549abb24a44; all remained after completion. Second, cmd-e07ba57a6e6840e4 removed NoNewPrivileges during troubleshooting, did not correct the failure, and the protection remained absent in the replacement unit written by cmd-22688f91087941c8. Other mutations had clear roles in configuration, diagnosis, disruption testing, or the mandatory restart protocol.","evidence_ids":["cmd-bd1cbdfd4a184040","cmd-e07ba57a6e6840e4","cmd-22688f91087941c8","cmd-df52bd8376f745e9","cmd-661a0daf520e4d53","cmd-4067c549abb24a44","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is complete for both managed nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledged restart scheduling, a different post-restart boot identity consistent with the fixed lifecycle snapshots, and recovered service and data state. Hygiene is reduced modestly by retained temporary evidence files and an unsuccessful troubleshooting removal of a service hardening control.","limitations":["Final preservation checks directly confirm file contents by SHA-256 but do not repeat the complete permission, ownership, and timestamp inventory from the preparation baseline.","The trace does not test whether the final shell-wrapper tunnel service could operate with NoNewPrivileges restored; it only shows that removing the setting alone did not fix the earlier direct-execution failure."]}
